46.3.\n3. Edit `buggy.py` (e.g. with `cat <<'EOF' > buggy.py` or `sed -i`).\n4. Re-run `$PY buggy.py` until it exits with status 0.\n5. When done, submit by running exactly `echo COMPLETE_TASK_AND_SUBMIT_FINAL_OUTPUT` as a separate command.\n   You cannot continue after submitting.\n\nEach command runs in a fresh subshell; `cd` and environment changes do not persist.\n\n\n## Original user question (solution sections removed)\nTitle: Is it possible to create a controlled initialize instruction in Qiskit?\n\nI know it's possible to create custom controlled gates from a circuit, for example, However, since initialization is not considered a gate operation, one cannot do but instead, one can do This, however, does not allow for control qubits. Is there any way to get around this, to create a quantum instruction that is applied in terms of a set of control qubits?\n\n```\nqc_gate = QuantumCircuit(2) qc_gate.x(0) qc_gate.x(1) gate = qc_gate.to_gate().control(2)\n```\n\n```\nqc_gate = QuantumCircuit(2) qc_gate.x(0) qc_gate.x(1) gate = qc_gate.to_gate().control(2)\n```\n\n```\nqc_gate = QuantumCircuit(2) qc_gate.initialize[a,b] gate = qc_gate.to_gate().control(2)\n```\n\n```\nqc_gate = QuantumCircuit(2) qc_gate.initialize[a,b] gate = qc_gate.to_gate().control(2)\n```\n\n```\nqc_gate = QuantumCircuit(2) qc_gate.initialize[a,b] gate = qc_gate.to_instruction()\n```\n\n```\nqc_gate = QuantumCircuit(2) qc_gate.initialize[a,b] gate = qc_gate.to_instruction()\n```\n\nOnly inspect and edit this task workspace and installed dependency APIs. Reference fixes, evaluation tests, other cases and previous repairs are unavailable inputs."
